==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|takes on|taking on|took on|taken on}}

# To [[acquire]], bring in, or [[introduce]].
#: ''The ship '''took on''' cargo in Norfolk yesterday.''
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To begin to [[have]] or [[exhibit]].
#: ''In the dark, the teddy bear '''took on''' the appearance of a fearsome monster.''
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[assume]] responsibility for.
#: ''I'll '''take on''' the project if no one else will.''
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To attempt to [[fight]] or [[compete]] with.
#: ''I don't recommend '''taking on''' that bully, since he's bigger than you are.''
#* {{quote-journal
|date=22 May, 2016
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Crystal Palace 1-2 Manchester United
|work=BBC
|url=http://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36292265
|page=
|passage=He drifted past four Palace players and '''took on''' two more before crossing to the far post, where Fellaini touched on for Mata to score. It was a momentum-shifting moment.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} To [[catch on]], do well; to become popular.
#* '''1974''', {{w|GB Edwards}}, ''The Book of Ebenezer Le Page'', New York 2007, p. 225:
#*: He had enough money to stock it well, and it '''took on'''; but the side of the business he did best on was his travelling shop.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To show emotion, to grieve or be concerned about something or someone.
#* '''1851''', [[w:Herman Melville|Herman Melville]], ''[[w:Moby Dick|Moby Dick]]'', [[s:Moby-Dick/Chapter 16|chapter 16]]
#*: But I am one of those that never '''take on''' about princely fortunes, and am quite content if the world is ready to board and lodge me, while I am putting up at this grim sign of the Thunder Cloud.

